        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is a good waist-to-hip ratio?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The World Health Organization treats a waist-to-hip ratio above 0.85 for women and above 0.90 for men as abdominal obesity. Below those lines is generally considered lower risk, but the ratio is one indicator among several, not a diagnosis."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is a good waist-to-height ratio?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Keeping your waist under half your height, a ratio below 0.50, is the common rule of thumb. The commonly used bands are 0.40 to 0.49 for the healthy range, 0.50 to 0.59 for increased risk and 0.60 or more for high risk."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What are ideal body proportions?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "There is no single ideal. Ratios such as the golden ratio, a waist-to-hip ratio near 0.7 or the proportions of the Vitruvian Man reflect cultural preferences rather than health. This calculator shows health reference points where they exist and treats aesthetic ratios as references, never as goals."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the Adonis index or golden ratio for the body?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The Adonis index is the ratio of shoulder circumference to waist circumference, with 1.618, the golden ratio, as the popular benchmark. It is a fitness and aesthetics idea with no proven health significance, so use it as a curiosity or a training reference only."
